	const line_number = ts_split`import { add } from "math"

const NUM = 100 // [!del]
const NUM = 50 // [!add]

const v = add(NUM, "hi") // [!err add...*]`;

	const syntax_demo = ts_split`import { add } from "./math" // [!dim]
// [!hl +2]
const price = 100;
const tax = 20;
const total = add(price, tax); // [!em price...tax]
log(total, total); // [!err =total]`;

	const html_output = html`<pre class="twinkleplop">
  <code>
    <span class="l"><span class="ln">1</span> ...tokens </span>
    <span class="l"><span class="ln">2</span> ...tokens</span>
    <span class="l"><span class="ln">3</span> ...tokens</span>
  </code>
</pre>`

	const css_output = css`.twinkleplop .ln {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 40px;
  text-align: right;
  padding-right: 16px;
}`
</script>

<ArticleMain
	pane_path="docs / directives"
	title="directives"
	subtitle="Use comments to highlight lines and text in a code snippet."

>
	<Section id="s1" title="intro" num="§ 01">
		Source directives add CSS classes to selected lines or text. They extend [Shiki's notation transformers](/docs/migration#notation) with more ways to select parts of a snippet.

		Write directives in comments in the source you want to highlight.

		Each directive has a verb, such as `hl` for highlighting, and optional arguments that select the lines or text it applies to.

		<SplitCodeBlock
			lang="typescript"
			left_html={line_number.input}
			right_html={line_number.output}
		/>
	</Section>

<Section id="syntax" title="syntax" num="§ 02">

A directive is `[!verb]` or `[!verb arguments]`, written inside a comment and closed on the same line. A verb starts with a letter and continues with letters, digits, `_` or `-`. The argument form decides what the directive applies to: line numbers select whole lines, anchors select text.

| form | mode | applies to |
| ---- | ---- | ---------- |
| `[!verb]` | line | the line the marker is on |
| `[!verb +N]` | line | the N lines below the marker |
| `[!verb :N]` | line | line N of the source |
| `[!verb :N..M]` | line | the lines between N and M, excluding both |
| `[!verb :N...M]` | line | lines N to M, including both |
| `[!verb a..b]` | token | the text between anchors `a` and `b` on the marker's line |
| `[!verb a...b]` | token | from `a` to `b` on the marker's line, including both |
| `[!verb ***]` | token | the code on the marker's line |
| `[!verb =a]` | token | every occurrence of `a` on the marker's line |
| `[!verb =a +N]` | token | every occurrence of `a` in the N lines below; `:N`, `:N..M` and `:N...M` work too |
| `[!verb =a :*]` | token | every occurrence of `a` in the snippet |
| `[!verb a...]` `[!verb ...b]` | token | a pair, from `a` on the opening marker's line to `b` on the closing marker's line; `..` excludes the anchors |
| `[!verb#id a...]` | token | names a pair, so pairs of the same verb can overlap |

There are three anchor formats:

| anchor | matches |
| ------ | ------- |
| `foo` | the whole word `foo`: letters, digits and `_`, so it does not match inside `foobar` |
| `"foo(bar"` | the exact text, spaces and punctuation included; `\"` and `\\` escape |
| `*` | the start of the line as the first anchor, the end of the line as the second |

- Anchors never match inside a comment, so a marker cannot match its own text.
- A line holding nothing but a marker comment is removed from the output, so a bare `[!verb]` has to follow code on the same line. `:N` line numbers still count removed lines.
- Unconfigured verbs are treated as ordinary comment text.
- A marker with arguments the parser cannot read stays in the output; one whose anchor is not found is removed and applies nothing. Both are reported to the `on_error` option of the annotation config, or with `console.warn` when it is not set.

Example using several directive formats:

<SplitCodeBlock
	lang="typescript"
	left_html={syntax_demo.input}
	right_html={syntax_demo.output}
/>

</Section>

<Section id="s2" title="using directives" num="§ 03">

Directives are disabled by default. Enable the ones you need in the highlighter configuration.

The built-in directives select line or token mode based on their arguments:

| plugin   | verb   | classification | typical use                          |
| -------- | ------ | -------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `em`     | `em`   | `emphasis`     | draw attention                       |
| `hl`     | `hl`   | `highlight`    | persistent highlight                 |
| `focus`  | `focus`| `focus`        | the lines to look at                 |
| `dim`    | `dim`  | `subdued`      | fade siblings                        |
| `add`    | `add`  | `diff-add`     | diff: added                          |
| `del`    | `del`  | `diff-del`     | diff: removed                        |
| `mod`    | `mod`  | `diff-mod`     | diff: modified                       |
| `err`    | `err`  | `error`        | diagnostic squiggle: error           |
| `warn`   | `warn` | `warning`      | diagnostic squiggle: warning         |
| `info`   | `info` | `info`         | diagnostic squiggle: info            |


</Section>

